#lang rhombus
// The frames a slide comes to rest on.
//
// A slide that has been given stages is an animated `Pict` -- a value from
// rhombus/pict, not a pict this runtime can read. A frame of it is a pict
// again, and the canvases it was built from are still inside that: the tag an
// element carries rides on the pict as a field, so every combinator between
// here and there has kept it.
//
// Which frame, though? Not simply the last: an epoch that fades something out
// ends on a picture with less in it than the one before, and a talk whose last
// beat is a fade-out would come back missing what it faded. So this offers
// every frame the slide settles on -- the start and the end of each epoch --
// and leaves the choice to the caller, which picks the one that shows the most.
//
// This is in Rhombus because `snapshot` is a Rhombus method; everything else
// that reads a slide is Racket, and calls this.
import: pict as pc

export:
  settle_frames
  stage_frames
  over_from

fun settle_frames(s):
  if s is_a pc.Pict
  | List.append(
      & (for List (i in 0 .. math.max(1, s.duration)):
           [s.snapshot(i, 1.0).handle, s.snapshot(i, 0.0).handle]))
  | [s]

// The pages a slide becomes in the show, in order.
//
// slideshow makes one page per epoch of an animated `Pict` -- `staged.rhm` says
// why: `s` steps a whole slide rather than an animation frame -- and each of
// those pages comes to rest at the end of its epoch. So a deck that steps the
// way the show steps holds one slide per epoch, and a slide that does not
// animate holds one.
fun stage_frames(s):
  if s is_a pc.Pict
  | for List (i in 0 .. math.max(1, s.duration)):
      s.snapshot(i, 1.0).handle
  | [s]

// A layer laid over a slide that animates, appearing from one stage on.
//
// A shape drawn on the third stage of a slide is a shape that appears there and
// not before, and the slide's own canvas cannot say so: a canvas is one still
// picture, and the staging is applied to it from outside. So the shape goes
// over the top, in a layer of its own that starts when it should -- built by
// `make_layer`, which is handed the slide's own size, so the layer is a canvas
// like any other and what it holds keeps its tag.
//
// `delay` is `time_clip` and `time_pad` together: nothing before the stage, and
// from there on whatever the layer draws.
fun over_from(stage, slide, make_layer):
  def a :~ pc.Pict = as_pict(slide)
  def layer :~ pc.Pict = as_pict(make_layer(a.width, a.height))
  // `concurrent` gives the two the same duration and hands them back as they
  // now stand; putting one over the other is a separate step, and it is left
  // and top so a layer the size of the slide lands where the slide is.
  def both = pc.concurrent(a, layer.delay(math.max(0, stage - 1)))
  pc.overlay(~horiz: #'left, ~vert: #'top, both[0], both[1])

fun as_pict(v) :~ pc.Pict:
  if v is_a pc.Pict | v | pc.Pict.from_handle(v)
